WEBVTT

00:00.760 --> 00:03.160
Use graphing to solve
the following problem.

00:05.770 --> 00:08.610
Abby and Ben did household
chores last weekend.

00:08.610 --> 00:13.690
Together they earned $50, and
Abby earned $10 more than Ben.

00:13.690 --> 00:15.480
How much did they each earn?

00:15.480 --> 00:16.990
So let's define
some variables here.

00:16.990 --> 00:21.780
Let's let A equal
Abby's earnings.

00:26.280 --> 00:30.495
And let's let B
equal Ben's earnings.

00:34.840 --> 00:37.500
Then they tell us how
these earnings relate.

00:37.500 --> 00:42.080
They first tell us that
together they earned $50.

00:42.080 --> 00:45.460
So that statement can be
converted mathematically

00:45.460 --> 00:46.890
into-- well,
together, that means

00:46.890 --> 00:48.280
the sum of the two earnings.

00:48.280 --> 00:53.800
So A plus B needs
to be equal to $50.

00:53.800 --> 00:56.590
Abby's plus Ben's
earnings is $50.

00:56.590 --> 01:01.640
And then they tell us Abby
earned $10 more than Ben.

01:01.640 --> 01:06.460
So we could translate
that into Abby's earnings

01:06.460 --> 01:09.290
is equal to Ben's
earnings plus 10.

01:09.290 --> 01:12.590
Abby earned $10 more than Ben.

01:12.590 --> 01:14.879
So we have a system
of two equations

01:14.879 --> 01:16.170
and actually with two unknowns.

01:16.170 --> 01:20.354
And then they say, how
much did each earn?

01:20.354 --> 01:22.770
So to do that, and they want
us to solve this graphically.

01:22.770 --> 01:24.186
There's multiple
ways to solve it,

01:24.186 --> 01:25.950
but we'll do what
they ask us to do.

01:25.950 --> 01:28.690
Let me draw some axes over here.

01:28.690 --> 01:30.160
And I'll be in
the first quadrant

01:30.160 --> 01:31.680
since we're dealing
with earnings,

01:31.680 --> 01:34.990
so neither of their
earnings can be negative.

01:34.990 --> 01:37.400
And let me just define
the vertical axis

01:37.400 --> 01:40.980
as Abby's axis or the
Abby's earnings axis.

01:40.980 --> 01:43.610
And let me define
the horizontal axis

01:43.610 --> 01:46.460
as Ben's axis or
Ben's earnings axis.

01:46.460 --> 01:49.420
And let me just graph
each of these equations.

01:49.420 --> 01:52.282
And to do that, I'm going
to take this first equation,

01:52.282 --> 01:53.990
and I'm going to put
it in the equivalent

01:53.990 --> 01:55.322
of slope-intercept form.

01:55.322 --> 01:57.030
It might look a little
unfamiliar to you,

01:57.030 --> 01:59.020
but it really is
slope-intercept form.

01:59.020 --> 02:00.020
Let me rewrite it first.

02:00.020 --> 02:03.180
So we have A plus
B is equal to 50.

02:03.180 --> 02:06.300
We can subtract B
from both sides.

02:06.300 --> 02:09.740
So let's subtract
B from both sides.

02:09.740 --> 02:17.920
And then we get A is equal
to negative B plus 50.

02:17.920 --> 02:20.830
So if you think about it this
way, when B is equal to 0,

02:20.830 --> 02:22.000
A is going to be 50.

02:22.000 --> 02:24.529
So we know our A intercept,
we could call it.

02:24.529 --> 02:26.820
We normally would call that
a y-intercept, but now this

02:26.820 --> 02:28.170
is the A axis.

02:28.170 --> 02:35.540
So this right here, let me call
this 10, 20, 30, 40, and 50.

02:35.540 --> 02:39.630
So if Ben made $0,
then Abby would

02:39.630 --> 02:42.800
have to make $50 based
on that first constraint.

02:42.800 --> 02:46.230
So we know that that's a point
on the line right over there.

02:46.230 --> 02:49.020
And we also know that
the slope is negative 1,

02:49.020 --> 02:51.560
that B is the independent
variable, the way I've written

02:51.560 --> 02:54.380
it over here, and this
coefficient is negative 1.

02:54.380 --> 02:58.540
Or another way to think
about it is if A is 0, then

02:58.540 --> 02:59.830
B is going to be 50.

02:59.830 --> 03:03.060
If Abby made no money, then
Ben would have to make $50.

03:03.060 --> 03:05.470
And that falls purely out
of this equation right here.

03:05.470 --> 03:06.970
If Abby made nothing,
then Ben would

03:06.970 --> 03:13.489
have to make $50, so
10, 20, 30, 40, 50.

03:13.489 --> 03:15.530
So those are those two
situations and every point

03:15.530 --> 03:18.640
in between will satisfy
this first constraint.

03:18.640 --> 03:21.440
So let me connect the dots.

03:21.440 --> 03:23.970
So it would look
something like that.

03:23.970 --> 03:26.190
That's due to this first
constraint, due to the fact

03:26.190 --> 03:29.077
that together they earned $50.

03:29.077 --> 03:30.660
Now, let's think
about the second one.

03:30.660 --> 03:33.140
Abby earned $10 more than Ben.

03:33.140 --> 03:35.090
So that's this
equation right here.

03:35.090 --> 03:37.530
And it's really already in
our slope-intercept form.

03:37.530 --> 03:41.490
If Ben made $0, then
Abby would make $10.

03:41.490 --> 03:42.720
So that's our A intercept.

03:42.720 --> 03:44.390
So it's right over there.

03:44.390 --> 03:45.680
We could keep doing that.

03:45.680 --> 03:47.020
Our slope is going to be 1 here.

03:47.020 --> 03:51.700
If Ben makes $10, then
Abby's going to make $20.

03:51.700 --> 03:56.574
If Ben makes $20, Abby's
going to make $30.

03:56.574 --> 03:58.490
We could keep going, but
I think this gives us

03:58.490 --> 03:59.406
the general direction.

03:59.406 --> 04:02.360
It already hints at a
point of intersection.

04:02.360 --> 04:05.480
So just eyeballing it, so we've
graphed to the two constraints,

04:05.480 --> 04:07.090
together they earned $50.

04:07.090 --> 04:09.480
That's the magenta
constraint right over here.

04:09.480 --> 04:11.810
Abby earned $10 more than Ben.

04:11.810 --> 04:14.417
That's this green
constraint right over here.

04:14.417 --> 04:16.500
And it looks like we have
a point of intersection.

04:16.500 --> 04:19.300
And it looks like we have
a point of intersection

04:19.300 --> 04:21.410
at Ben earning $20.

04:21.410 --> 04:25.540
Let me label this as
10, 20, 30, 40, and 50.

04:25.540 --> 04:29.460
So this is Ben earning
$20 and Abby earning--

04:29.460 --> 04:32.870
this is 10, 20, and 30.

04:32.870 --> 04:35.190
And Abby earning $30.

04:35.190 --> 04:40.940
So just eyeballing it off of
this, it looks like A is 30

04:40.940 --> 04:42.430
and B is 20.

04:42.430 --> 04:46.610
And let's go verify, make sure
that these levels of earnings

04:46.610 --> 04:50.370
for Abby and Ben actually
satisfy both constraints.

04:50.370 --> 04:54.030
So the first constraint is that
Abby plus Ben have to make $50.

04:54.030 --> 04:56.600
Well, $30 plus $20 is $50.

04:56.600 --> 04:58.550
So it meets our
first constraint.

04:58.550 --> 05:01.910
The second constraint is that
Abby earned $10 more than Ben,

05:01.910 --> 05:04.370
that Abby is equal
to Ben plus 10.

05:04.370 --> 05:05.810
Well, once again,
over here, Abby

05:05.810 --> 05:07.720
is making $10 more than Ben.

05:07.720 --> 05:09.970
So it meets our
second constraint.

05:09.970 --> 05:11.669
And we only have two of them.

05:11.669 --> 05:12.710
So it meets both of them.

05:12.710 --> 05:14.190
So that's our solution.

05:14.190 --> 05:15.740
Abby earned $30.

05:15.740 --> 05:17.833
Ben earned $20.
